{"surah": 49, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ لَا تُقَدِّمُوا۟ بَيْنَ يَدَىِ ٱللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِۦۖ وَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ سَمِيعٌ عَلِيمٌ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, do not put [yourselves] before Allāh and His Messenger[1] but fear Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is Hearing and Knowing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تُقَدِّمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmِّdَqُt", "root": "قدم", "latinTranslit": "tuqaddimū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "bayna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَدَىِ", "translit": "ِàَdَy", "root": "يدي", "latinTranslit": "yadayi"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَرَسُولِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlwُsَrَw", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "warasūlihi"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "wa-ittaqū"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "سَمِيعٌ", "translit": "ٌعyِmَs", "root": "سمع", "latinTranslit": "samīʿun"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَع", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "ʿalīmun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Believers, do not push yourselves forward in the presence of God and His Messenger––be mindful of God: He hears and knows all––", "clearyTranslation": "Believers, do not push yourselves forward in the presence of God and His Messenger––be mindful of God: He hears and knows all––"}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ لَا تَرْفَعُوٓا۟ أَصْوَٰتَكُمْ فَوْقَ صَوْتِ ٱلنَّبِىِّ وَلَا تَجْهَرُوا۟ لَهُۥ بِٱلْقَوْلِ كَجَهْرِ بَعْضِكُمْ لِبَعْضٍ أَن تَحْبَطَ أَعْمَٰلُكُمْ وَأَنتُمْ لَا تَشْعُرُونَ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet or be loud to him in speech like the loudness of some of you to others, lest your deeds become worthless while you perceive not.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَرْفَعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُعَfْrَt", "root": "رفع", "latinTranslit": "tarfaʿū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَصْوَٰتَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَtَٰwْṣَaʾ", "root": "صوت", "latinTranslit": "aṣwātakum"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَوْقَ", "translit": "َqْwَf", "root": "فوق", "latinTranslit": "fawqa"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صَوْتِ", "translit": "ِtْwَṣ", "root": "صوت", "latinTranslit": "ṣawti"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلنَّبِىِّ", "translit": "ِّàِbَّnlٱ", "root": "نبا", "latinTranslit": "l-nabiyi"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَجْهَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَhْjgَt", "root": "جهر", "latinTranslit": "tajharū"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِٱلْقَوْلِ", "translit": "ِlْwَqْlٱِb", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "bil-qawli"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَجَهْرِ", "translit": "ِrْhَjgَk", "root": "جهر", "latinTranslit": "kajahri"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بَعْضِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِḍْعَb", "root": "بعض", "latinTranslit": "baʿḍikum"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لِبَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْعَbِl", "root": "بعض", "latinTranslit": "libaʿḍin"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "تَحْبَطَ", "translit": "َṭَbْḥَt", "root": "حبط", "latinTranslit": "taḥbaṭa"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُlَٰmْعَaʾ", "root": "عمل", "latinTranslit": "aʿmālukum"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَأَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-antum"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "تَشْعُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُعْhsَt", "root": "شعر", "latinTranslit": "tashʿurūna"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "believers, do not raise your voices above the Prophet’s, do not raise your voice when speaking to him as you do to one another, or your [good] deeds may be cancelled out without you knowing", "clearyTranslation": "believers, do not raise your voices above the Prophet’s, do not raise your voice when speaking to him as you do to one another, or your [good] deeds may be cancelled out without you knowing"}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ يَغُضُّونَ أَصْوَٰتَهُمْ عِندَ رَسُولِ ٱللَّهِ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ ٱمْتَحَنَ ٱللَّهُ قُلُوبَهُمْ لِلتَّقْوَىٰۚ لَهُم مَّغْفِرَةٌ وَأَجْرٌ عَظِيمٌ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who lower their voices before the Messenger of Allāh - they are the ones whose hearts Allāh has tested for righteousness. For them is forgiveness and great reward.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَغُضُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّḍُغَy", "root": "غضض", "latinTranslit": "yaghuḍḍūna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَصْوَٰتَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَtَٰwْṣَaʾ", "root": "صوت", "latinTranslit": "aṣwātahum"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِع", "root": "عند", "latinTranslit": "ʿinda"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَسُولِ", "translit": "ِlwُsَr", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "rasūli"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ulāika"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱمْتَحَنَ", "translit": "َnَḥَtْmٱ", "root": "محن", "latinTranslit": "im'taḥana"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "قُلُوبَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَbwُlُq", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "qulūbahum"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لِلتَّقْوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwْqَّtlِl", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "lilttaqwā"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahum"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مَّغْفِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِfْغَّm", "root": "غفر", "latinTranslit": "maghfiratun"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَأَجْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْjgَaʾَw", "root": "اجر", "latinTranslit": "wa-ajrun"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَظِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِẓَع", "root": "عظم", "latinTranslit": "ʿaẓīmun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "It is those who lower their voices in the presence of God’s Messenger whose hearts God has proved to be aware––they will have forgiveness, and a great reward––", "clearyTranslation": "It is those who lower their voices in the presence of God’s Messenger whose hearts God has proved to be aware––they will have forgiveness, and a great reward––"}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ يُنَادُونَكَ مِن وَرَآءِ ٱلْحُجُرَٰتِ أَكْثَرُهُمْ لَا يَعْقِلُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who call you, [O Muḥammad], from behind the chambers - most of them do not use reason.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُنَادُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُdāَnُy", "root": "ندي", "latinTranslit": "yunādūnaka"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَرَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَrَw", "root": "وري", "latinTranslit": "warāi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْحُجُرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrُjgُḥْlٱ", "root": "حجر", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥujurāti"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَكْثَرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrَhtْkَaʾ", "root": "كثر", "latinTranslit": "aktharuhum"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَعْقِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِqْعَy", "root": "عقل", "latinTranslit": "yaʿqilūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "but most of those who shout to you [Prophet] from outside your private rooms lack understanding", "clearyTranslation": "but most of those who shout to you [Prophet] from outside your private rooms lack understanding"}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَلَوْ أَنَّهُمْ صَبَرُوا۟ حَتَّىٰ تَخْرُجَ إِلَيْهِمْ لَكَانَ خَيْرًا لَّهُمْۚ وَٱللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ ", "translation": "And if they had been patient until you [could] come out to them, it would have been better for them. But Allāh is Forgiving and Merciful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walaw"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "annahum"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "صَبَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَbَṣ", "root": "صبر", "latinTranslit": "ṣabarū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ḥattā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَخْرُجَ", "translit": "َjgُrْhkَt", "root": "خرج", "latinTranslit": "takhruja"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilayhim"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَl", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "lakāna"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير", "latinTranslit": "khayran"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahum"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "wal-lahu"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "غَفُورٌ", "translit": "ٌrwُfَغ", "root": "غفر", "latinTranslit": "ghafūrun"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رَّحِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِḥَّr", "root": "رحم", "latinTranslit": "raḥīmun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "It would have been better for them if they had waited patiently for you to come out to them but God is all forgiving and merciful", "clearyTranslation": "It would have been better for them if they had waited patiently for you to come out to them but God is all forgiving and merciful"}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ إِن جَآءَكُمْ فَاسِقٌۢ بِنَبَإٍ فَتَبَيَّنُوٓا۟ أَن تُصِيبُوا۟ قَوْمًۢا بِجَهَٰلَةٍ فَتُصْبِحُوا۟ عَلَىٰ مَا فَعَلْتُمْ نَٰدِمِينَ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, if there comes to you a disobedient one with information, investigate, lest you harm a people out of ignorance and become, over what you have done, regretful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "in"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "جَآءَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا", "latinTranslit": "jāakum"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَاسِقٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢqِsāَf", "root": "فسق", "latinTranslit": "fāsiqun"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِنَبَإٍ", "translit": "ٍiʾَbَnِb", "root": "نبا", "latinTranslit": "binaba-in"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَتَبَيَّنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَّyَbَtَf", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "fatabayyanū"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تُصِيبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbyِṣُt", "root": "صوب", "latinTranslit": "tuṣībū"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَوْمًۢا", "translit": "āًۢmْwَq", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "qawman"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِجَهَٰلَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَlَٰhَjgِb", "root": "جهل", "latinTranslit": "bijahālatin"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَتُصْبِحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِbْṣُtَf", "root": "صبح", "latinTranslit": "fatuṣ'biḥū"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَعَلْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْlَعَf", "root": "فعل", "latinTranslit": "faʿaltum"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "نَٰدِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِdَٰn", "root": "ندم", "latinTranslit": "nādimīna"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Believers, if a troublemaker brings you news, check it first, in case you wrong others unwittingly and later regret what you have done", "clearyTranslation": "Believers, if a troublemaker brings you news, check it first, in case you wrong others unwittingly and later regret what you have done"}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَٱعْلَمُوٓا۟ أَنَّ فِيكُمْ رَسُولَ ٱللَّهِۚ لَوْ يُطِيعُكُمْ فِى كَثِيرٍ مِّنَ ٱلْأَمْرِ لَعَنِتُّمْ وَلَٰكِنَّ ٱللَّهَ حَبَّبَ إِلَيْكُمُ ٱلْإِيمَٰنَ وَزَيَّنَهُۥ فِى قُلُوبِكُمْ وَكَرَّهَ إِلَيْكُمُ ٱلْكُفْرَ وَٱلْفُسُوقَ وَٱلْعِصْيَانَۚ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلرَّٰشِدُونَ ", "translation": "And know that among you is the Messenger of Allāh. If he were to obey you in much of the matter, you would be in difficulty, but Allāh has endeared to you the faith and has made it pleasing in your hearts and has made hateful to you disbelief, defiance and disobedience. Those are the [rightly] guided.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱعْلَمُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُmَlْعٱَw", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "wa-iʿ'lamū"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "anna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِيكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīkum"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَسُولَ", "translit": "َlwُsَr", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "rasūla"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "law"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُطِيعُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُعyِṭُy", "root": "طوع", "latinTranslit": "yuṭīʿukum"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَثِيرٍ", "translit": "ٍryِhtَk", "root": "كثر", "latinTranslit": "kathīrin"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر", "latinTranslit": "l-amri"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَعَنِتُّمْ", "translit": "ْmُّtِnَعَl", "root": "عنت", "latinTranslit": "laʿanittum"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِkَٰlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walākinna"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "حَبَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّbَḥ", "root": "حبب", "latinTranslit": "ḥabbaba"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilaykumu"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْإِيمَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰmyِiʾْlٱ", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "l-īmāna"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَزَيَّنَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnَّyَzَw", "root": "زين", "latinTranslit": "wazayyanahu"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "قُلُوبِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِbwُlُq", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "qulūbikum"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَكَرَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّrَkَw", "root": "كره", "latinTranslit": "wakarraha"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilaykumu"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلْكُفْرَ", "translit": "َrْfُkْlٱ", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "l-kuf'ra"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَٱلْفُسُوقَ", "translit": "َqwُsُfْlٱَw", "root": "فسق", "latinTranslit": "wal-fusūqa"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَٱلْعِصْيَانَ", "translit": "َnāَyْṣِعْlٱَw", "root": "عصي", "latinTranslit": "wal-ʿiṣ'yāna"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ulāika"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "humu"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "ٱلرَّٰشِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِhsَّٰrlٱ", "root": "رشد", "latinTranslit": "l-rāshidūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "and be aware that it is God’s Messenger who is among you: in many matters you would certainly suffer if he were to follow your wishes. God has endeared faith to you and made it beautiful to your hearts; He has made disbelief, mischief, and disobedience hateful to you. It is people like this who are rightly guided", "clearyTranslation": "and be aware that it is God’s Messenger who is among you: in many matters you would certainly suffer if he were to follow your wishes. God has endeared faith to you and made it beautiful to your hearts; He has made disbelief, mischief, and disobedience hateful to you. It is people like this who are rightly guided"}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "فَضْلًا مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ وَنِعْمَةًۚ وَٱللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ حَكِيمٌ ", "translation": "[It is] as bounty from Allāh and favor. And Allāh is Knowing and Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَضْلًا", "translit": "āًlْḍَf", "root": "فضل", "latinTranslit": "faḍlan"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَنِعْمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmْعِnَw", "root": "نعم", "latinTranslit": "waniʿ'matan"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "wal-lahu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَع", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "ʿalīmun"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "حَكِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِkَḥ", "root": "حكم", "latinTranslit": "ḥakīmun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "through God’s favour and blessing: God is all knowing and all wise", "clearyTranslation": "through God’s favour and blessing: God is all knowing and all wise"}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "وَإِن طَآئِفَتَانِ مِنَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ ٱقْتَتَلُوا۟ فَأَصْلِحُوا۟ بَيْنَهُمَاۖ فَإِنۢ بَغَتْ إِحْدَىٰهُمَا عَلَى ٱلْأُخْرَىٰ فَقَٰتِلُوا۟ ٱلَّتِى تَبْغِى حَتَّىٰ تَفِىٓءَ إِلَىٰٓ أَمْرِ ٱللَّهِۚ فَإِن فَآءَتْ فَأَصْلِحُوا۟ بَيْنَهُمَا بِٱلْعَدْلِ وَأَقْسِطُوٓا۟ۖ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُحِبُّ ٱلْمُقْسِطِينَ ", "translation": "And if two factions among the believers should fight, then make settlement between the two. But if one of them oppresses the other, then fight against the one that oppresses until it returns to the ordinance of Allāh. And if it returns, then make settlement between them in justice and act justly. Indeed, Allāh loves those who act justly.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-in"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "طَآئِفَتَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَtَfِyʾٓāَṭ", "root": "طوف", "latinTranslit": "ṭāifatāni"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "l-mu'minīna"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱقْتَتَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَtَtْqٱ", "root": "قتل", "latinTranslit": "iq'tatalū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَأَصْلِحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِlْṣَaʾَf", "root": "صلح", "latinTranslit": "fa-aṣliḥū"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "baynahumā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَإِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِiʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-in"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَغَتْ", "translit": "ْtَغَb", "root": "بغي", "latinTranslit": "baghat"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِحْدَىٰهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhٰàَdْḥِiʾ", "root": "احد", "latinTranslit": "iḥ'dāhumā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأُخْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhkُaʾْlٱ", "root": "اخر", "latinTranslit": "l-ukh'rā"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَقَٰتِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِtَٰqَf", "root": "قتل", "latinTranslit": "faqātilū"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "allatī"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تَبْغِى", "translit": "àِغْbَt", "root": "بغي", "latinTranslit": "tabghī"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ḥattā"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "تَفِىٓءَ", "translit": "َءٓàِfَt", "root": "فيا", "latinTranslit": "tafīa"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر", "latinTranslit": "amri"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "فَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-in"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "فَآءَتْ", "translit": "ْtَءٓāَf", "root": "فيا", "latinTranslit": "fāat"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "فَأَصْلِحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِlْṣَaʾَf", "root": "صلح", "latinTranslit": "fa-aṣliḥū"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "baynahumā"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "بِٱلْعَدْلِ", "translit": "ِlْdَعْlٱِb", "root": "عدل", "latinTranslit": "bil-ʿadli"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَأَقْسِطُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُṭِsْqَaʾَw", "root": "قسط", "latinTranslit": "wa-aqsiṭū"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "يُحِبُّ", "translit": "ُّbِḥُy", "root": "حبب", "latinTranslit": "yuḥibbu"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ٱلْمُقْسِطِينَ", "translit": "َnyِṭِsْqُmْlٱ", "root": "قسط", "latinTranslit": "l-muq'siṭīna"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "If two groups of the believers fight, you [believers] should try to reconcile them; if one of them is [clearly] oppressing the other, fight the oppressors until they submit to God’s command, then make a just and even-handed reconciliation between the two of them: God loves those who are even-handed", "clearyTranslation": "If two groups of the believers fight, you [believers] should try to reconcile them; if one of them is [clearly] oppressing the other, fight the oppressors until they submit to God’s command, then make a just and even-handed reconciliation between the two of them: God loves those who are even-handed"}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "إِنَّمَا ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ إِخْوَةٌ فَأَصْلِحُوا۟ بَيْنَ أَخَوَيْكُمْۚ وَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُرْحَمُونَ ", "translation": "The believers are but brothers, so make settlement between your brothers. And fear Allāh that you may receive mercy.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "innamā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "l-mu'minūna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِخْوَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَwْhkِiʾ", "root": "اخو", "latinTranslit": "ikh'watun"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأَصْلِحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِlْṣَaʾَf", "root": "صلح", "latinTranslit": "fa-aṣliḥū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "bayna"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَخَوَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَwَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخو", "latinTranslit": "akhawaykum"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "wa-ittaqū"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَعَلَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّlَعَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "laʿallakum"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تُرْحَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَḥْrُt", "root": "رحم", "latinTranslit": "tur'ḥamūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "The believers are brothers, so make peace between your two brothers and be mindful of God, so that you may be given mercy", "clearyTranslation": "The believers are brothers, so make peace between your two brothers and be mindful of God, so that you may be given mercy"}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ لَا يَسْخَرْ قَوْمٌ مِّن قَوْمٍ عَسَىٰٓ أَن يَكُونُوا۟ خَيْرًا مِّنْهُمْ وَلَا نِسَآءٌ مِّن نِّسَآءٍ عَسَىٰٓ أَن يَكُنَّ خَيْرًا مِّنْهُنَّۖ وَلَا تَلْمِزُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَكُمْ وَلَا تَنَابَزُوا۟ بِٱلْأَلْقَٰبِۖ بِئْسَ ٱلِٱسْمُ ٱلْفُسُوقُ بَعْدَ ٱلْإِيمَٰنِۚ وَمَن لَّمْ يَتُبْ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, let not a people ridicule [another] people; perhaps they may be better than them; nor let women ridicule [other] women; perhaps they may be better than them. And do not insult one another and do not call each other by [offensive] nicknames. Wretched is the name [i.e., mention] of disobedience after [one's] faith. And whoever does not repent - then it is those who are the wrongdoers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَسْخَرْ", "translit": "ْrَhkْsَy", "root": "سخر", "latinTranslit": "yaskhar"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَوْمٌ", "translit": "ٌmْwَq", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "qawmun"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَq", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "qawmin"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَsَع", "root": "عسي", "latinTranslit": "ʿasā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَكُونُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnwُkَy", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "yakūnū"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير", "latinTranslit": "khayran"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min'hum"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "نِسَآءٌ", "translit": "ٌءٓāَsِn", "root": "نسو", "latinTranslit": "nisāon"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "نِّسَآءٍ", "translit": "ٍءٓāَsِّn", "root": "نسو", "latinTranslit": "nisāin"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "عَسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَsَع", "root": "عسي", "latinTranslit": "ʿasā"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يَكُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُkَy", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "yakunna"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير", "latinTranslit": "khayran"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مِّنْهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhْnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min'hunna"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "تَلْمِزُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُzِmْlَt", "root": "لمز", "latinTranslit": "talmizū"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "أَنفُسَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس", "latinTranslit": "anfusakum"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "تَنَابَزُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُzَbāَnَt", "root": "نبز", "latinTranslit": "tanābazū"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "بِٱلْأَلْقَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰqْlَaʾْlٱِb", "root": "لقب", "latinTranslit": "bil-alqābi"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "بِئْسَ", "translit": "َsْyʾِb", "root": "باس", "latinTranslit": "bi'sa"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ٱلِٱسْمُ", "translit": "ُmْsٱِlٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-s'mu"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "ٱلْفُسُوقُ", "translit": "ُqwُsُfْlٱ", "root": "فسق", "latinTranslit": "l-fusūqu"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "بَعْدَ", "translit": "َdْعَb", "root": "بعد", "latinTranslit": "baʿda"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "ٱلْإِيمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmyِiʾْlٱ", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "l-īmāni"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waman"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "لَّمْ", "translit": "ْmَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lam"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "يَتُبْ", "translit": "ْbُtَy", "root": "توب", "latinTranslit": "yatub"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-ulāika"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "humu"}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم", "latinTranslit": "l-ẓālimūna"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Believers, no one group of men should jeer at another, who may after all be better than them; no one group of women should jeer at another, who may after all be better than them; do not speak ill of one another; do not use offensive nicknames for one another. How bad it is to be called a mischief-maker after accepting faith! Those who do not repent of this behaviour are evildoers", "clearyTranslation": "Believers, no one group of men should jeer at another, who may after all be better than them; no one group of women should jeer at another, who may after all be better than them; do not speak ill of one another; do not use offensive nicknames for one another. How bad it is to be called a mischief-maker after accepting faith! Those who do not repent of this behaviour are evildoers"}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ ٱجْتَنِبُوا۟ كَثِيرًا مِّنَ ٱلظَّنِّ إِنَّ بَعْضَ ٱلظَّنِّ إِثْمٌۖ وَلَا تَجَسَّسُوا۟ وَلَا يَغْتَب بَّعْضُكُم بَعْضًاۚ أَيُحِبُّ أَحَدُكُمْ أَن يَأْكُلَ لَحْمَ أَخِيهِ مَيْتًا فَكَرِهْتُمُوهُۚ وَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ تَوَّابٌ رَّحِيمٌ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, avoid much [negative] assumption. Indeed, some assumption is sin. And do not spy or backbite each other. Would one of you like to eat the flesh of his brother when dead? You would detest it. And fear Allāh; indeed, Allāh is Accepting of Repentance and Merciful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱجْتَنِبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbِnَtْjgٱ", "root": "جنب", "latinTranslit": "ij'tanibū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَثِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِhtَk", "root": "كثر", "latinTranslit": "kathīran"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلظَّنِّ", "translit": "ِّnَّẓlٱ", "root": "ظنن", "latinTranslit": "l-ẓani"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَعْضَ", "translit": "َḍْعَb", "root": "بعض", "latinTranslit": "baʿḍa"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلظَّنِّ", "translit": "ِّnَّẓlٱ", "root": "ظنن", "latinTranslit": "l-ẓani"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِثْمٌ", "translit": "ٌmْhtِiʾ", "root": "اثم", "latinTranslit": "ith'mun"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَجَسَّسُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُsَّsَjgَt", "root": "جسس", "latinTranslit": "tajassasū"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَغْتَب", "translit": "bَtْغَy", "root": "غيب", "latinTranslit": "yaghtab"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بَّعْضُكُم", "translit": "mُkُḍْعَّb", "root": "بعض", "latinTranslit": "baʿḍukum"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بَعْضًا", "translit": "āًḍْعَb", "root": "بعض", "latinTranslit": "baʿḍan"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَيُحِبُّ", "translit": "ُّbِḥُyَaʾ", "root": "حبب", "latinTranslit": "ayuḥibbu"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَحَدُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد", "latinTranslit": "aḥadukum"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "يَأْكُلَ", "translit": "َlُkْaʾَy", "root": "اكل", "latinTranslit": "yakula"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "لَحْمَ", "translit": "َmْḥَl", "root": "لحم", "latinTranslit": "laḥma"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "أَخِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِhkَaʾ", "root": "اخو", "latinTranslit": "akhīhi"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مَيْتًا", "translit": "āًtْyَm", "root": "موت", "latinTranslit": "maytan"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "فَكَرِهْتُمُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُmُtْhِrَkَf", "root": "كره", "latinTranslit": "fakarih'tumūhu"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "wa-ittaqū"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "تَوَّابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَّwَt", "root": "توب", "latinTranslit": "tawwābun"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "رَّحِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِḥَّr", "root": "رحم", "latinTranslit": "raḥīmun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Believers, avoid making too many assumptions- some assumptions are sinful- and do not spy on one another or speak ill of people behind their backs: would any of you like to eat the flesh of your dead brother? No, you would hate it. So be mindful of God: God is ever relenting, most merciful", "clearyTranslation": "Believers, avoid making too many assumptions- some assumptions are sinful- and do not spy on one another or speak ill of people behind their backs: would any of you like to eat the flesh of your dead brother? No, you would hate it. So be mindful of God: God is ever relenting, most merciful"}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ إِنَّا خَلَقْنَٰكُم مِّن ذَكَرٍ وَأُنثَىٰ وَجَعَلْنَٰكُمْ شُعُوبًا وَقَبَآئِلَ لِتَعَارَفُوٓا۟ۚ إِنَّ أَكْرَمَكُمْ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ أَتْقَىٰكُمْۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ عَلِيمٌ خَبِيرٌ ", "translation": "O mankind, indeed We have created you from male and female and made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another. Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of Allāh is the most righteous[1] of you. Indeed, Allāh is Knowing and Aware.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "innā"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَٰكُم", "translit": "mُkَٰnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق", "latinTranslit": "khalaqnākum"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ذَكَرٍ", "translit": "ٍrَkَhd", "root": "ذكر", "latinTranslit": "dhakarin"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأُنثَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhtnُaʾَw", "root": "انث", "latinTranslit": "wa-unthā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَٰnْlَعَjgَw", "root": "جعل", "latinTranslit": "wajaʿalnākum"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شُعُوبًا", "translit": "āًbwُعُhs", "root": "شعب", "latinTranslit": "shuʿūban"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَقَبَآئِلَ", "translit": "َlِyʾٓāَbَqَw", "root": "قبل", "latinTranslit": "waqabāila"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لِتَعَارَفُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُfَrāَعَtِl", "root": "عرف", "latinTranslit": "litaʿārafū"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَكْرَمَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَmَrْkَaʾ", "root": "كرم", "latinTranslit": "akramakum"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِع", "root": "عند", "latinTranslit": "ʿinda"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَتْقَىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَqْtَaʾ", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "atqākum"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَع", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "ʿalīmun"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "خَبِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِbَhk", "root": "خبر", "latinTranslit": "khabīrun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "People, We created you all from a single man and a single woman, and made you into races and tribes so that you should recognize one another. In God’s eyes, the most honoured of you are the ones most mindful of Him: God is all knowing, all aware", "clearyTranslation": "People, We created you all from a single man and a single woman, and made you into races and tribes so that you should recognize one another. In God’s eyes, the most honoured of you are the ones most mindful of Him: God is all knowing, all aware"}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "قَالَتِ ٱلْأَعْرَابُ ءَامَنَّاۖ قُل لَّمْ تُؤْمِنُوا۟ وَلَٰكِن قُولُوٓا۟ أَسْلَمْنَا وَلَمَّا يَدْخُلِ ٱلْإِيمَٰنُ فِى قُلُوبِكُمْۖ وَإِن تُطِيعُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُۥ لَا يَلِتْكُم مِّنْ أَعْمَٰلِكُمْ شَيْـًٔاۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ ", "translation": "The bedouins say, \"We have believed.\" Say, \"You have not [yet] believed; but say [instead], 'We have submitted,' for faith has not yet entered your hearts. And if you obey Allāh and His Messenger, He will not deprive you from your deeds[1] of anything. Indeed, Allāh is Forgiving and Merciful.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَتِ", "translit": "ِtَlāَq", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "qālati"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَعْرَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَrْعَaʾْlٱ", "root": "عرب", "latinTranslit": "l-aʿrābu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmannā"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "qul"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَّمْ", "translit": "ْmَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lam"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تُؤْمِنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnِmْwʾُt", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "tu'minū"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walākin"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قُولُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlwُq", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "qūlū"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَسْلَمْنَا", "translit": "āَnْmَlْsَaʾ", "root": "سلم", "latinTranslit": "aslamnā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walammā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَدْخُلِ", "translit": "ِlُhkْdَy", "root": "دخل", "latinTranslit": "yadkhuli"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْإِيمَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰmyِiʾْlٱ", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "l-īmānu"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "قُلُوبِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِbwُlُq", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "qulūbikum"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-in"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "تُطِيعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُعyِṭُt", "root": "طوع", "latinTranslit": "tuṭīʿū"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَرَسُولَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlwُsَrَw", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "warasūlahu"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يَلِتْكُم", "translit": "mُkْtِlَy", "root": "ليت", "latinTranslit": "yalit'kum"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِlَٰmْعَaʾ", "root": "عمل", "latinTranslit": "aʿmālikum"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "shayan"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "غَفُورٌ", "translit": "ٌrwُfَغ", "root": "غفر", "latinTranslit": "ghafūrun"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "رَّحِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِḥَّr", "root": "رحم", "latinTranslit": "raḥīmun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "The desert Arabs say, ‘We have faith.’ [Prophet], tell them, ‘You do not have faith. What you should say instead is, “We have submitted,” for faith has not yet entered your hearts.’ If you obey God and His Messenger, He will not diminish any of your deeds: He is most forgiving and most merciful", "clearyTranslation": "The desert Arabs say, ‘We have faith.’ [Prophet], tell them, ‘You do not have faith. What you should say instead is, “We have submitted,” for faith has not yet entered your hearts.’ If you obey God and His Messenger, He will not diminish any of your deeds: He is most forgiving and most merciful"}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "إِنَّمَا ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِۦ ثُمَّ لَمْ يَرْتَابُوا۟ وَجَٰهَدُوا۟ بِأَمْوَٰلِهِمْ وَأَنفُسِهِمْ فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِۚ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلصَّٰدِقُونَ ", "translation": "The believers are only the ones who have believed in Allāh and His Messenger and then doubt not but strive with their properties and their lives in the cause of Allāh. It is those who are the truthful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "innamā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "l-mu'minūna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "bil-lahi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَرَسُولِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlwُsَrَw", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "warasūlihi"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lam"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَرْتَابُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbāَtْrَy", "root": "ريب", "latinTranslit": "yartābū"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَجَٰهَدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdَhَٰjgَw", "root": "جهد", "latinTranslit": "wajāhadū"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِأَمْوَٰلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlَٰwْmَaʾِb", "root": "مول", "latinTranslit": "bi-amwālihim"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَأَنفُسِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِsُfnَaʾَw", "root": "نفس", "latinTranslit": "wa-anfusihim"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل", "latinTranslit": "sabīli"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ulāika"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "humu"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰدِقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqِdَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صدق", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣādiqūna"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "The true believers are the ones who have faith in God and His Messenger and leave all doubt behind, the ones who have struggled with their possessions and their persons in God’s way: they are the ones who are true", "clearyTranslation": "The true believers are the ones who have faith in God and His Messenger and leave all doubt behind, the ones who have struggled with their possessions and their persons in God’s way: they are the ones who are true"}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "قُلْ أَتُعَلِّمُونَ ٱللَّهَ بِدِينِكُمْ وَٱللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِۚ وَٱللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَىْءٍ عَلِيمٌ ", "translation": "Say, \"Would you acquaint Allāh with your religion while Allāh knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth, and Allāh is Knowing of all things?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "qul"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَتُعَلِّمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِّlَعُtَaʾ", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "atuʿallimūna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِدِينِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِnyِdِb", "root": "دين", "latinTranslit": "bidīnikum"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "wal-lahu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْعَy", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "yaʿlamu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-samāwāti"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "l-arḍi"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "wal-lahu"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِb", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "bikulli"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "shayin"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَع", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "ʿalīmun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Say, ‘Do you presume to teach God about your religion, when God knows everything in the heavens and earth, and He has full knowledge of all things?’", "clearyTranslation": "Say, ‘Do you presume to teach God about your religion, when God knows everything in the heavens and earth, and He has full knowledge of all things?’"}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "يَمُنُّونَ عَلَيْكَ أَنْ أَسْلَمُوا۟ۖ قُل لَّا تَمُنُّوا۟ عَلَىَّ إِسْلَٰمَكُمۖ بَلِ ٱللَّهُ يَمُنُّ عَلَيْكُمْ أَنْ هَدَىٰكُمْ لِلْإِيمَٰنِ إِن كُنتُمْ صَٰدِقِينَ ", "translation": "They consider it a favor to you that they have accepted Islām. Say, \"Do not consider your Islām a favor to me. Rather, Allāh has conferred favor upon you that He has guided you to the faith, if you should be truthful.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَمُنُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّnُmَy", "root": "منن", "latinTranslit": "yamunnūna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalayka"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنْ", "translit": "ْnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَسْلَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَlْsَaʾ", "root": "سلم", "latinTranslit": "aslamū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "qul"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَمُنُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّnُmَt", "root": "منن", "latinTranslit": "tamunnū"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalayya"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِسْلَٰمَكُم", "translit": "mُkَmَٰlْsِiʾ", "root": "سلم", "latinTranslit": "is'lāmakum"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَلِ", "translit": "ِlَb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bali"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَمُنُّ", "translit": "ُّnُmَy", "root": "منن", "latinTranslit": "yamunnu"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalaykum"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَنْ", "translit": "ْnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "هَدَىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَdَh", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "hadākum"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لِلْإِيمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmyِiʾْlِl", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "lil'īmāni"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "in"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "kuntum"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "صَٰدِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِdَٰṣ", "root": "صدق", "latinTranslit": "ṣādiqīna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "They think they have done you [Prophet] a favour by submitting. Say, ‘Do not consider your submission a favour to me; it is God who has done you a favour, by guiding you to faith, if you are truly sincere.’", "clearyTranslation": "They think they have done you [Prophet] a favour by submitting. Say, ‘Do not consider your submission a favour to me; it is God who has done you a favour, by guiding you to faith, if you are truly sincere.’"}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ غَيْبَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِۚ وَٱللَّهُ بَصِيرٌۢ بِمَا تَعْمَلُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, Allāh knows the unseen [aspects] of the heavens and the earth. And Allāh is Seeing of what you do.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْعَy", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "yaʿlamu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "غَيْبَ", "translit": "َbْyَغ", "root": "غيب", "latinTranslit": "ghayba"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-samāwāti"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "wal-arḍi"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "wal-lahu"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَصِيرٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢryِṣَb", "root": "بصر", "latinTranslit": "baṣīrun"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bimā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْعَt", "root": "عمل", "latinTranslit": "taʿmalūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "God knows the secrets of the heavens and earth: He sees everything you do", "clearyTranslation": "God knows the secrets of the heavens and earth: He sees everything you do"}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذل", "english": "And in the ḥadīth of Abān, from Anas ibn Mālik — may Allah be pleased with him: \"Blessed (ṭūbā) is a servant who spends from wealth he earned without disobedience.\" And if what he puts forth is not from the finest of his wealth, then that is from ill-manners (sūʾ al-adab), since he may have kept the finest for himself, or for his servant, or for his family — and so he has preferred over Allah — Exalted is He — someone other than Him. Were he to do this with his guest and offer him the worst food in his house, that would kindle rage in his breast. This is if his regard is toward Allah — Exalted is He. And if his regard is toward himself and his reward in the Hereafter, then he is not intelligent who prefers others over himself — for he has nothing of his wealth save what he gave in charity (so it remains for him) or what he ate (so it is consumed). And what he eats is merely the satisfaction of a need in the present moment. It is not from intelligence to confine one's regard to the immediate (al-ʿājilah) and to abandon laying up provision (al-iddikhār). Allah — Exalted is He — has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه»} (\"O you who believe, spend from the good things you have earned and from what We have brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim at the bad thereof to spend — while you would not take it yourselves save with closed eyes\") — that is, you would not take it save with aversion and shame, and that is the meaning of closing the eyes (al-ighmāḍ). So do not prefer over your Lord that which you yourselves would disdain.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "2": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذل", "english": "And in the ḥadīth of Abān, from Anas ibn Mālik — may Allah be pleased with him: \"Blessed (ṭūbā) is a servant who spends from wealth he earned without disobedience.\" And if what he puts forth is not from the finest of his wealth, then that is from ill-manners (sūʾ al-adab), since he may have kept the finest for himself, or for his servant, or for his family — and so he has preferred over Allah — Exalted is He — someone other than Him. Were he to do this with his guest and offer him the worst food in his house, that would kindle rage in his breast. This is if his regard is toward Allah — Exalted is He. And if his regard is toward himself and his reward in the Hereafter, then he is not intelligent who prefers others over himself — for he has nothing of his wealth save what he gave in charity (so it remains for him) or what he ate (so it is consumed). And what he eats is merely the satisfaction of a need in the present moment. It is not from intelligence to confine one's regard to the immediate (al-ʿājilah) and to abandon laying up provision (al-iddikhār). Allah — Exalted is He — has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه»} (\"O you who believe, spend from the good things you have earned and from what We have brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim at the bad thereof to spend — while you would not take it yourselves save with closed eyes\") — that is, you would not take it save with aversion and shame, and that is the meaning of closing the eyes (al-ighmāḍ). So do not prefer over your Lord that which you yourselves would disdain.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "3": [{"arabic": "تعالى أولئك الذين امتحن الله قلوبهم للتقوى قيل نزع منها محبة الشهوات وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم المؤمن بين خمس شدائد مؤمن يحسده ومنافق يبغضه وكافر يقاتله وشيطان يضله ونفس تنازعه (1) فبين أن النفس عدو منازع يجب عليه مجاهدتها ويروى أن الله تعالى أوحى إلى داود عليه السلام يا داود حذر وأنذر أصحابك أكل الشهوات فإن القلوب المتعلقة بشهوات الدنيا عقولها عني محجوبة وقال عيسى عليه السلام طوبى لمن ترك شهوة حاضرة لموعود غائب لم يره وقال نبينا", "english": "And that which necessitates faith in this matter — from the Qur'an, the Sunnah, and the sayings of the scholars — is more than can be enumerated. God, the Exalted, said: {«ونهى النفس عن الهوى فإن الجنة هي المأوى»} — \"And he who restrained his soul from caprice — verily Paradise is the abode.\" And He, the Exalted, said: {«أولئك الذين امتحن الله قلوبهم للتقوى»} — \"Those are they whose hearts God has tested for piety\" — and it was said: He removed from them the love of the desires. And the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"The believer is between five severe trials: a believer who envies him, a hypocrite who hates him, an unbeliever who fights him, a devil who leads him astray, and a soul that contends with him.\" Thus he made clear that the soul is a contending enemy whose struggle (mujāhadah) is incumbent upon him.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 627", "section_title_en": "Section 627 — The Path of Opposition to the Desires: On the Consensus of the Scholars that There Is No Road to the Happiness of the Hereafter Save through Restraining the Soul from Caprice", "section_index": 627, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit; that which breathes; the animating principle; also blood, and the \"evil eye\" (nafs sā'ilah)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس: الروح — the soul as the spiritual reality; in the verse {«ويحذركم الله نفسه»} it denotes the very Essence (al-dhāt); the soul is that which must be disciplined and opposed in its caprice (hawā)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self that desires and inclines; Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" — he poured out his blood/life; the soul as the internal adversary that must be fought (jihād al-nafs)\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; to turn over, to transform, to fluctuate; the heart as that which is constantly turning between states\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and fluctuation between conditions; the seat of spiritual perception and disease\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to turn something over; Metaphorical: the heart as the organ of spiritual understanding that can be veiled (maḥjūbah) from God by attachment to desires\n- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be near, proximity; the world (al-dunyā) as that which is nearer/lower\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الدنو: القرب بالذات أو بالحكم — proximity by essence or by judgment; the world as the nearer realm that veils from the distant (al-ākhirah)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: closeness; Metaphorical: the lower world as that which is \"near\" and thus preoccupies the heart from what is higher\n- Root: أكل (a-k-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To eat, consume; also to possess and enjoy\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Eating as the most fundamental desire; the restriction of food (qillat al-ṭa'ām) as the first pillar of spiritual discipline (riyāḍah)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: consumption of food; Metaphorical: \"أكل الشهوات\" — the devouring of desires as that which veils the intellect from God\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic sentence structure in which the author interposes explanatory clauses between the subject and its predicate, as in \"فإنه لو تمتع بشيء منه أنس به وألفه\" where the conditional clause governs the entire following apodosis, requiring careful tracking of the protasis-apodosis relationship through extended digressions.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "6": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذل", "english": "And in the ḥadīth of Abān, from Anas ibn Mālik — may Allah be pleased with him: \"Blessed (ṭūbā) is a servant who spends from wealth he earned without disobedience.\" And if what he puts forth is not from the finest of his wealth, then that is from ill-manners (sūʾ al-adab), since he may have kept the finest for himself, or for his servant, or for his family — and so he has preferred over Allah — Exalted is He — someone other than Him. Were he to do this with his guest and offer him the worst food in his house, that would kindle rage in his breast. This is if his regard is toward Allah — Exalted is He. And if his regard is toward himself and his reward in the Hereafter, then he is not intelligent who prefers others over himself — for he has nothing of his wealth save what he gave in charity (so it remains for him) or what he ate (so it is consumed). And what he eats is merely the satisfaction of a need in the present moment. It is not from intelligence to confine one's regard to the immediate (al-ʿājilah) and to abandon laying up provision (al-iddikhār). Allah — Exalted is He — has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه»} (\"O you who believe, spend from the good things you have earned and from what We have brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim at the bad thereof to spend — while you would not take it yourselves save with closed eyes\") — that is, you would not take it save with aversion and shame, and that is the meaning of closing the eyes (al-ighmāḍ). So do not prefer over your Lord that which you yourselves would disdain.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا إن جاءكم فاسق بنبأ فتبينوا أن تصيبوا قوما بجهالة فلا يجوز تصديق إبليس وإن كان ثم مخيلة تدل على فساد واحتمل خلافه لم يجز أن تصدق به لأن الفاسق يتصور أن بصدق في خيره ولكن لا يجوز لك أن تصدق به حتى إن من استنكه فوجد منه رائحة الخمر لا يجوز أن يحد إذ يقال يمكن أن يكون قد تمضمض بالخمر ومجها وما شربها أو حمل عليه قهرا فكل ذلك لا محالة دلالة محتملة فلا يجوز تصديقها بالقلب", "english": "Know that evil suspicion (sūʾ al-ẓann) is forbidden just as evil speech is forbidden. For just as it is unlawful for you to speak to others with your tongue about another's faults, so too you have no right to speak to yourself and think evil of your brother. By this I mean nothing other than the heart's settled conviction (ʿaqd al-qalb) and its judgment of evil upon another. As for passing thoughts (khaṭarāt) and self-talk (ḥadīth al-nafs), they are pardoned. Nay, even doubt (shakk) is pardoned. But what is prohibited is that you \"think\" (ẓann) — and ẓann is an expression for that which the soul rests upon and toward which the heart inclines.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 687", "section_title_en": "The Cure That Restrains the Tongue from Backbiting, and the Prohibition of Backbiting in the Heart", "section_index": 687, "anchors": "- Root: غيب (ghayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: That which is hidden from the eye; absence from sensory perception.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ghaybah (backbiting) is rooted in speaking of one who is absent (ghāʾib) — the unseen referent is absent from the listener's sight, yet present in the speaker's malice.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"speaking from behind the veil of absence\" (takallama bihi ʿan ẓahri al-ghayb). Metaphorical: the tongue's slander reaches the absent one as if traversing a hidden realm.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self; the vital spirit; that which breathes and desires.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The nafs is the locus of blameworthy traits (hawā) and the battlefield between angelic and satanic promptings; its thoughts (khaṭarāt) are distinct from settled convictions (ʿaqd al-qalb).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"self-speaking\" (ḥadīth al-nafs) as internal discourse. Metaphorical: the nafs \"inclines\" (tamīlu) toward suspicion — a leaning of the heart, not a mere passing thought.\n- Root: قلب (qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; the organ of understanding and intention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Taqlīb — the heart's constant turning between states; its \"settling\" (ʿaqd) upon a judgment constitutes belief (iʿtiqād), which is the object of divine prohibition in suspicion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: turning a thing over. Metaphorical: the heart \"turns over\" a matter until it rests upon a conviction — this resting (sukūn) is what renders suspicion sinful, unlike the fleeting whisper.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and specification (bāb al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — the phrase \"ولست أعني به إلا عقد القلب\" restricts the referent of \"suspicion\" exclusively to the heart's settled judgment, excluding passing thoughts (khaṭarāt) and doubt (shakk), which are pardoned.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "9": [{"arabic": "الله فسوف نؤتيه أجرا عظيما وقال تعالى وإن طائفتان من المؤمنين اقتتلوا فأصلحوا بينهما الآية والإصلاح نهي عن البغي وإعادة إلى الطاعة فإن لم يفعل فقد أمر الله تعالى بقتاله فقال فقاتلوا التي تبغي حتى تفيء إلى أمر الله وذلك هو النهي عن المنكر وأما الأخبار فمنها ما روي عن أبي بكر الصديق رضي الله عنه أنه قال في خطبة خطبها أيها الناس إنكم تقرءون هذه الآية وتؤولونها على خلاف تأويلها يا أيها الذين آمنوا عليكم أنفسكم لا يضركم من ضل إذا اهتديتم (1) وإني سمعت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "فقد أمر الله تعالى بقتاله فقال فقاتلوا التي تبغي حتى تفيء إلى أمر الله وذلك هو النهي عن المنكر وأما الأخبار فمنها ما روي عن أبي بكر الصديق رضي الله عنه أنه قال في خطبة خطبها أيها الناس إنكم تقرءون هذه الآية وتؤولونها على خلاف تأويلها يا أيها الذين آمنوا عليكم أنفسكم لا يضركم من ضل إذا اهتديتم (1) وإني سمعت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول ما من قوم عملوا بالمعاصي وفيهم من يقدر أن ينكر عليهم فلم يفعل إلا يوشك أن يعمهم الله بعذاب من عنده وروى عن أبي ثعلبة الخشني أنه س", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "11": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذل", "english": "And in the ḥadīth of Abān, from Anas ibn Mālik — may Allah be pleased with him: \"Blessed (ṭūbā) is a servant who spends from wealth he earned without disobedience.\" And if what he puts forth is not from the finest of his wealth, then that is from ill-manners (sūʾ al-adab), since he may have kept the finest for himself, or for his servant, or for his family — and so he has preferred over Allah — Exalted is He — someone other than Him. Were he to do this with his guest and offer him the worst food in his house, that would kindle rage in his breast. This is if his regard is toward Allah — Exalted is He. And if his regard is toward himself and his reward in the Hereafter, then he is not intelligent who prefers others over himself — for he has nothing of his wealth save what he gave in charity (so it remains for him) or what he ate (so it is consumed). And what he eats is merely the satisfaction of a need in the present moment. It is not from intelligence to confine one's regard to the immediate (al-ʿājilah) and to abandon laying up provision (al-iddikhār). Allah — Exalted is He — has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه»} (\"O you who believe, spend from the good things you have earned and from what We have brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim at the bad thereof to spend — while you would not take it yourselves save with closed eyes\") — that is, you would not take it save with aversion and shame, and that is the meaning of closing the eyes (al-ighmāḍ). So do not prefer over your Lord that which you yourselves would disdain.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}, {"arabic": "إلا الحياة الدنيا وفي قوله تعالى ومن لم يتب فأولئك هم الظالمون إلى غير ذلك من الآيات وكان داخلا في معنى قوله عز وجل ومنهم أميون لا يعلمون الكتاب إلا أماني يعني التلاوة المجردة وقوله عز وجل وكأين من آية في السموات والأرض يمرون عليها وهم عنها معرضون لأن القرآن هو المبين لتلك الآيات في السموات والأرض ومهما تجاوزها ولم يتأثر بها كان معرضا عنها ولذلك قيل إن من لم يكن متصفا بأخلاق القرآن فإذا قرأ القرآن ناداه الله تعالى مالك ولكلامي وأنت معرض عني دع عنك كلامي إن", "english": "And among the fearful there were those who fell down unconscious at the verses of threat, and among them were those who died upon hearing the verses. Such states as these remove a person from being merely a narrator in his speech. Thus, if one says, \"Indeed, I fear, if I disobey my Lord, the chastisement of a tremendous Day\" (39:13) — while not being fearful — he is merely narrating. And if one says, \"Upon You we rely, and to You we turn in repentance, and to You is the final return\" (60:4) — while his state is not one of reliance and repentance — he is merely narrating. And if one says, \"And we will surely endure patiently whatever harm you inflict upon us\" (14:12) — then his state must be one of patience or of firm resolve upon it, so that he may taste the sweetness of recitation.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 230", "section_title_en": "The States of the Heart During Qur'anic Recitation — The Condition of the True Recieter", "section_index": 230, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of trembling fear, trepidation of the heart\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the inward apprehension of fear; cited in {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a man fearful, a people fearful; the state of the heart's trembling before divine majesty\n- Root: وصف (w-ṣ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Describing a thing by its attributes and qualities\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوصف: ذكر الشيء بحليته ونعته\" — description is the mention of a thing by its adornment and attribute; may be true or false\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: \"وجهها يصف الحسن\" — metaphorical usage where the face itself \"describes\" beauty; likewise, the tongue may \"describe\" falsehood, as in {ولا تقولوا لما تصف ألسنتكم الكذب}\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, reaching full measure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which attains completion; \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — fulfilled the covenant without breach\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham; Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة إذا زاد عليها\" — surpassed the hundred\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The separation between the governing preposition and its object by intervening clauses, as in \"فلما بلغت {فكيف إذا جئنا...}\" where the temporal clause governs the narrative sequence", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "وهذا محرم مهما كان مؤذيا كما قال تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا لا يسخر قوم من قوم عسى أن يكونوا خيرا منهم ولا نساء من نساء عسى أن يكن خيرا منهن ومعنى السخرية الاستهانة والتحقير والتنبيه على العيوب والنقائص على وجه يضحك منه وقد يكون ذلك بالمحاكاة في الفعل والقول وقد يكون بالإشارة والإيماء وإذا كان بحضرة المستهزأ به لم يسم ذلك غيبة وفيه معنى الغيبة قالت عائشة رضي الله عنها حاكيت إنسانا فقال لي النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم والله ما أحب أني حاكيت إنسانا ولي كذا وكذا (2) وقال ابن عباس في قوله تعالى يا ويلتنا ما لهذا الكتاب لا يغادر صغيرة ولا كبير", "english": "This is forbidden whenever it is harmful, just as God—Exalted is He—has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا لا يسخر قوم من قوم عسى أن يكونوا خيرا منهم ولا نساء من نساء عسى أن يكن خيرا منهن»} [\"O you who believe, let not a people deride a people; perhaps they may be better than them. Nor let women deride women; perhaps they may be better than them\"] (Q. 49:11). The meaning of mockery (al-sukhriyyah) is contempt, belittlement, and drawing attention to faults and defects in a manner that causes laughter at the person. This may occur through imitation in action and speech, and it may occur through gesture and indication. And if it occurs in the presence of the one being mocked, it is not called backbiting (ghībah), yet it contains the meaning of backbiting.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 673", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 673: The Eleventh Affliction: Mockery and Derision", "section_index": 673, "anchors": "- Root: سخر (s-kh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of subjugation and forced employment; also derision and making a mockery of someone.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التسخير: سياقة إلى الغرض المختص قهرا\" — driving toward a specific purpose by compulsion. The theological nuance here shifts to the passive form (سُخِرَ) and the verbal noun (سخرية) denoting the act of holding another in contempt and compelling them into a position of ridicule.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (Literal): \"فلان سخرة سخرة: يضحك منه الناس\" — one whom people laugh at. Majaz (Metaphorical): The root's use for subjugated service (as in \"سخر الله لك\") is distinct from this interpersonal derision, which is the literal sense intended here.\n- Root: ضحك (ḍ-ḥ-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Physical laughter with the baring of teeth; the molars are called الضواحك.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الضحك: انبساط الوجه وتكشر الأسنان من سرور النفس\" — expansion of the face and baring of teeth from delight of the soul. He explicitly notes: \"واستعير الضحك للسخرية\" — laughter is metaphorically extended to mockery, as in \"ضحكت منه\" (I laughed at him). This metaphorical extension is precisely the mechanism of the sin discussed here.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"افتر عن ضاحكته\" — smiling broadly. Majaz: \"ضحكت الأرض عن النبات\" — the earth \"laughs\" forth vegetation. The distinction is critical: the physical act of laughing (haqiqah) becomes blameworthy mockery (majaz) when directed at another's defects.\n- Root: هلم (h-l-m)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"هلم دعاء إلى الشيء\" — a call toward something. Derived from \"ها لم\" (come, repair to) or \"هل أم\" (is there intent?). It is an imperative summons.\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"لأَن الهاء زائدة، وأَصله هالُمّ\" — the ha is augmentative; the root is l-m-m (to repair, to mend).\n- Root: وقد (w-q-d)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"وقدت النار تقد وقودا ووقدا\" — the fire blazed. The verbal noun وقود refers both to fuel and to the resulting flame.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"وقدت النار وقودا ووقدا، واتقدت وتوقدت\" — the fire ignited and blazed. Majaz: \"قلب وقاد\" — a blazing heart (for intelligence). The imagery of a door opening to the scorner, who approaches \"بكربه وغمه\" (with his distress and grief), evokes this root's sense of burning anguish.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The prohibition is restricted by the conditional clause \"مهما كان مؤذيا\" (whenever it is harmful), and the focus is sharpened by the Quranic address \"يا أيها الذين آمنوا\" which restricts the address to believers, making the prohibition binding upon them specifically.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "12": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذل", "english": "And in the ḥadīth of Abān, from Anas ibn Mālik — may Allah be pleased with him: \"Blessed (ṭūbā) is a servant who spends from wealth he earned without disobedience.\" And if what he puts forth is not from the finest of his wealth, then that is from ill-manners (sūʾ al-adab), since he may have kept the finest for himself, or for his servant, or for his family — and so he has preferred over Allah — Exalted is He — someone other than Him. Were he to do this with his guest and offer him the worst food in his house, that would kindle rage in his breast. This is if his regard is toward Allah — Exalted is He. And if his regard is toward himself and his reward in the Hereafter, then he is not intelligent who prefers others over himself — for he has nothing of his wealth save what he gave in charity (so it remains for him) or what he ate (so it is consumed). And what he eats is merely the satisfaction of a need in the present moment. It is not from intelligence to confine one's regard to the immediate (al-ʿājilah) and to abandon laying up provision (al-iddikhār). Allah — Exalted is He — has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه»} (\"O you who believe, spend from the good things you have earned and from what We have brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim at the bad thereof to spend — while you would not take it yourselves save with closed eyes\") — that is, you would not take it save with aversion and shame, and that is the meaning of closing the eyes (al-ighmāḍ). So do not prefer over your Lord that which you yourselves would disdain.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}, {"arabic": "ومن أبوابه سوء الظن بالمسلمين قال الله تعالى {يا أيها الذين آمنوا اجتنبوا كثيرا من الظن إن بعض الظن إثم} فمن يحكم بشر على غيره بالظن بعثه الشيطان على أن يطول فيه اللسان بالغيبة فيهلك أو يقصر في القيام بحقوقه أو يتوانى في إكرامه وينظر إليه بعين الاحتقار ويرى نفسه خيرا منه وكل ذلك من المهلكات ولأجل ذلك منع الشرع من التعرض للتهم فقال صلى الله عليه وسلم اتقوا مواضع التهم (2) حديث صفية بنت حيى أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم كان معتكفا فأتيته فتحدثت عنده الحديث وفيه أن الشطيان يجري من ابن آدم مجرى الدم متفق عليه فان", "english": "And among his entrances is thinking evil of Muslims. God — exalted — said: {«يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اجْتَنِبُوا كَثِيرًا مِّنَ الظَّنِّ إِنَّ بَعْضَ الظَّنِّ إِثْمٌ»} (O you who believe, avoid much suspicion — indeed, some suspicion is sin). For whoever judges evil of another by mere suspicion, the Shayṭān incites him to lengthen his tongue against him in backbiting — and so he perishes — or to fall short in fulfilling his rights, or to be negligent in honoring him, looking upon him with the eye of contempt and seeing himself as better than him. And all of that is among the destroyers. For this reason, the Sacred Law prohibited exposing oneself to suspicion, as he said — peace be upon him: \"Beware of places of suspicion.\" The ḥadīth of Ṣafiyya bint Ḥuyayy — that the Prophet — peace be upon him — was in seclusion (iʿtikāf), and she came to him and spoke with him — the ḥadīth — and in it: \"The Shayṭān runs through the son of Adam like the running of blood\" — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh).", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 602", "section_title_en": "On the Devil's Entrances: Wealth, Partisanship, and the Corruption of Hearts", "section_index": 602, "anchors": "- Root: شيط (shayṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of burning, scorching, or spoiling by fire; also one who is destroyed or whose blood is shed in vain.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Shayṭān is that which is far removed from good and rebellious among jinn and men.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: meat that is burnt and spoiled. Metaphorical: one whose blood is shed in vain (shāṭa damuhu), or one who is torn apart and divided like sacrificial meat (asḥāṭū laḥm al-jazūr).\n- Root: حدث (ḥadath)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: New occurrence, event, or innovation; that which comes into being after not having been.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ḥudūth is the coming-to-be of a thing after it was not — whether accident or substance. Iḥdāth is bringing it into existence. The Prophet's advent was a \"new event\" (ḥadath).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: recent, newly occurred. Metaphorical: \"Umar was muḥaddath — one whose intuition is true, as if he were informed (yuḥaddath) of hidden matters.\n- Root: عصب (ʿaṣab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To bind, tie firmly; partisanship (taʿaṣṣub) — the binding of oneself to a group or faction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ʿAṣabiyyah — zealous partisanship for one's group, whether in truth or falsehood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: binding a limb with a bandage. Metaphorical: binding oneself to a party or faction in blind allegiance.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd) — distinguishing genuine claims from false ones through predicate focus and contrastive emphasis.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "ولا تجسسوا ولا يغتب بعضكم بعضا وكم زاهد جاهل يوحش القلوب في التفتيش ويتكلم الكلام الخشن المؤذي وإنما يحسن الشيطان ذلك عنده طلبا للشهرة بأكل الحلال ولو كان باعثه محض الدين لكان خوفه على قلب مسلم أن يتأذى أشد من خوفه على بطنه أن يدخله ما لا يدري وهو غير مؤاخذ بما لا يدري إذ لم يكن ثم علامة توجب الاجتناب فليعلم أن طريق الورع الترك دون التجسس وإذا لم يكن بد من الأكل فالورع الأكل وإحسان الظن هذا هو المألوف من الصحابة رضي الله عنهم ومن ز", "english": "If you say: \"Perhaps he will not be offended\" — I say: \"Perhaps he will be offended.\" You ask out of caution against a \"perhaps.\" Then be content with the \"perhaps\" that his wealth is lawful. For the sin feared in injuring a Muslim is no less than the sin in eating the ambiguous (al-shubhah) and the unlawful. And the prevalent disposition among people is alienation at being investigated. It is not permissible for him to ask others about him — from where he himself knows — because the injury in that is greater. And if he asks from where he does not know, then in that is ill opinion, tearing of the veil, spying (tajassus), and clinging to backbiting (ghībah), even if it is not explicit. And all of that is prohibited in one verse — God Most High said: {«اجْتَنِبُوا كَثِيرًا مِنَ الظَّنِّ إِنَّ بَعْضَ الظَّنِّ إِثْمٌ ۖ وَلَا تَجَسَّسُوا وَلَا يَغْتَبْ بَعْضُكُمْ بَعْضًا»} (\"Avoid much suspicion — indeed, some suspicion is a sin — and do not spy, and do not backbite one another\") {al-Ḥujurāt: 12}.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 366", "section_title_en": "The Ruling on the Unknown Provider — Between Scrupulousness (Waraʿ) and Suspicion", "section_index": 366, "anchors": "- Root: ورع (w-r-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of restraint, abstaining, and holding back from what is doubtful or forbidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Waraʿ is the virtue of the God-fearing — abstaining not only from the unlawful but from the ambiguous (shubhah), as a safeguard for religion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage denotes physical withdrawal; metaphorical usage denotes moral abstention — the heart's recoil from what is suspect.\n- Root: ظن (ẓ-n-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Uncertainty, conjecture, or opinion that falls short of certainty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ẓann is divided into blameworthy suspicion (sūʾ al-ẓann) against the innocent — which is prohibited — and praiseworthy presumption of good (ḥusn al-ẓann) toward a Muslim, which is obligated by brotherhood in faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to hold an opinion. Metaphorical: the heart's inclination toward one of two possibilities without decisive evidence.\n- Root: ريبة (r-y-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Doubt, unease, or suspicion arising from a tangible indicator — not mere whim.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Rayb is the disturbance of certainty by an ambiguous circumstance; it is the legitimate trigger for inquiry, unlike baseless suspicion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncertainty. Metaphorical: the inward disquiet that prompts a man to investigate before acting.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The conditional and exceptive structures here (e.g., \"لا يلزمك السؤال\" and \"إلا عن ريبة\") restrict the obligation of inquiry to the presence of a genuine ground for doubt, not to mere possibility.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "13": [{"arabic": "من الخنازير ولذلك قال تعالى {يا أيها الناس إنا خلقناكم من ذكر وأنثى} أي لا تفاوت في أنسابكم لاجتماعكم في أصل واحد ثم ذكر فائدة النسب فقال {وجعلناكم شعوبا وقبائل لتعارفوا} ثم بين أن الشرف بالتقوى لا بالنسب فقال {إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم} ولما قيل لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم من أكرم الناس من أكيس الناس لم يقل من ينتمي إلى نسبي ولكن قال أكرمهم أكثرهم للموت ذكرا وأشدهم له استعدادا (1) حديث إن الله قد أذهب عنكم عيبة الجاهلية الحديث أخرجه أبو داود والترمذي وحسنه من حديث أبي ه", "english": "The fourth: pride in noble lineage, like the pride of the Hashimite, to the point that some of them imagine that they will be saved by the nobility of their lineage and the salvation of their forefathers, and that they are forgiven; and some of them fancy that all of creation are their clients and slaves. Its remedy is that he should know that whenever he contradicts his forefathers in their deeds and their moral traits, yet imagines himself to be joined with them, he has indeed been ignorant. And if he emulates his forefathers, then their moral traits were not pride, but rather fear, self-contempt, magnifying others, and self-blame. For they were ennobled by obedience, knowledge, and praiseworthy qualities — not by lineage. So let him seek honor through that by which they were honored. For those who shared their lineage and participated with them in their tribes, yet did not believe in God and the Last Day, were in God's sight worse than dogs and viler than swine. For this reason God (Exalted is He) said: {«يا أيها الناس إنا خلقناكم من ذكر وأنثى»} — \"O mankind, indeed We have created you from a male and a female\" — that is, there is no disparity in your lineages because you all share a single origin. Then He mentioned the benefit of lineage, saying: {«وجعلناكم شعوبا وقبائل لتعارفوا»} — \"And We made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another.\" Then He clarified that honor is by piety, not by lineage, saying: {«إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم»} — \"Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of God is the most righteous of you.\" And when it was said to the Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him), \"Who is the most noble of people?\" — he did not say, \"Whoever traces himself to my lineage,\" but rather he said, \"The most noble of them is the one most mindful of death and the most prepared for it.\" (1) A hadith: \"Indeed, God has removed from you the pride of the Age of Ignorance\" — this hadith was transmitted by Abu Dawud and al-Tirmidhi, who deemed it sound (ḥasan), from the narration of Abu Hurayrah; al-Tirmidhi also narrated it from the narration of Ibn ʿUmar and said it is gharīb (uncommon).", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 881", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 881: On the Wonders of Pride in Strength, Intellect, and Lineage, and Their Remedies", "section_index": 881, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faculty of restraint and comprehension; the intellect as that which binds one from error.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — the power prepared for receiving knowledge, and the knowledge acquired thereby.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sound judgment after folly; Metaphorical: \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm that does not accept pollination, i.e., lacks receptivity.\n- Root: نسب (nasab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Lineage and kinship through forebears.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النسب والنسبة: اشتراك من جهة أحد الأبوين\" — participation through one of the two parents; two types: vertical (through ancestors) and horizontal (through collaterals).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فلان حسيب نسيب\" — noble in deed and lineage; Metaphorical: \"بين الشيئين مناسبة\" — proportionality or correspondence between things.\n- Root: حدث (ḥadath)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; newness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — a thing's coming to be after it was not; \"المحدث: ما أوجد بعد أن لم يكن\" — the originated.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: recent events; Metaphorical: \"كان عمر رضي الله عنه محدثا\" — one inspired with true discernment.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which animates.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the spirit; also the very essence (dhāt) when attributed to God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: preciousness; Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" — poured out his blood, i.e., his life.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, applied here to parse the conditional and relative clauses conveying causal relations between pride and its consequences.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "{وجعلناكم شعوبا وقبائل لتعارفوا} ثم بين أن الشرف بالتقوى لا بالنسب فقال {إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم} ولما قيل لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم من أكرم الناس من أكيس الناس لم يقل من ينتمي إلى نسبي ولكن قال أكرمهم أكثرهم للموت ذكرا وأشدهم له استعدادا (1) حديث إن الله قد أذهب عنكم عيبة الجاهلية الحديث أخرجه أبو داود والترمذي وحسنه من حديث أبي هريرة ورواه الترمذي أيضا من حديث ابن عمر وقال غريب وقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم يا معشر قريش لا تأتي", "english": "The fourth: pride in noble lineage, like the pride of the Hashimite, to the point that some of them imagine that they will be saved by the nobility of their lineage and the salvation of their forefathers, and that they are forgiven; and some of them fancy that all of creation are their clients and slaves. Its remedy is that he should know that whenever he contradicts his forefathers in their deeds and their moral traits, yet imagines himself to be joined with them, he has indeed been ignorant. And if he emulates his forefathers, then their moral traits were not pride, but rather fear, self-contempt, magnifying others, and self-blame. For they were ennobled by obedience, knowledge, and praiseworthy qualities — not by lineage. So let him seek honor through that by which they were honored. For those who shared their lineage and participated with them in their tribes, yet did not believe in God and the Last Day, were in God's sight worse than dogs and viler than swine. For this reason God (Exalted is He) said: {«يا أيها الناس إنا خلقناكم من ذكر وأنثى»} — \"O mankind, indeed We have created you from a male and a female\" — that is, there is no disparity in your lineages because you all share a single origin. Then He mentioned the benefit of lineage, saying: {«وجعلناكم شعوبا وقبائل لتعارفوا»} — \"And We made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another.\" Then He clarified that honor is by piety, not by lineage, saying: {«إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم»} — \"Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of God is the most righteous of you.\" And when it was said to the Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him), \"Who is the most noble of people?\" — he did not say, \"Whoever traces himself to my lineage,\" but rather he said, \"The most noble of them is the one most mindful of death and the most prepared for it.\" (1) A hadith: \"Indeed, God has removed from you the pride of the Age of Ignorance\" — this hadith was transmitted by Abu Dawud and al-Tirmidhi, who deemed it sound (ḥasan), from the narration of Abu Hurayrah; al-Tirmidhi also narrated it from the narration of Ibn ʿUmar and said it is gharīb (uncommon).", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 881", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 881: On the Wonders of Pride in Strength, Intellect, and Lineage, and Their Remedies", "section_index": 881, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faculty of restraint and comprehension; the intellect as that which binds one from error.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — the power prepared for receiving knowledge, and the knowledge acquired thereby.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sound judgment after folly; Metaphorical: \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm that does not accept pollination, i.e., lacks receptivity.\n- Root: نسب (nasab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Lineage and kinship through forebears.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النسب والنسبة: اشتراك من جهة أحد الأبوين\" — participation through one of the two parents; two types: vertical (through ancestors) and horizontal (through collaterals).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فلان حسيب نسيب\" — noble in deed and lineage; Metaphorical: \"بين الشيئين مناسبة\" — proportionality or correspondence between things.\n- Root: حدث (ḥadath)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; newness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — a thing's coming to be after it was not; \"المحدث: ما أوجد بعد أن لم يكن\" — the originated.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: recent events; Metaphorical: \"كان عمر رضي الله عنه محدثا\" — one inspired with true discernment.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which animates.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the spirit; also the very essence (dhāt) when attributed to God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: preciousness; Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" — poured out his blood, i.e., his life.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, applied here to parse the conditional and relative clauses conveying causal relations between pride and its consequences.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "الحسب والشرف والنسب فنظرت فيها فإذا هي لا شيء ثم نظرت إلى قول الله تعالى إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم فعملت في التقوى حتى أكون عند الله كريما الخامسة أني نظرت إلى هذا الخلق وهم يطعن بعضهم في بعض ويلعن بعضهم بعضا وأصل هذا كله الحسد ثم نظرت إلى قول الله عز وجل نحن قسمنا بينهم معيشتهم في الحياة الدنيا فتركت الحسد واجتنبت الخلق وعلمت أن القسمة من عند الله سبحانه وتعالى فتركت عداوة الخلق عني السادسة نظرت إلى هذا الخلق يبغي بعضهم على بعض ويقاتل بعضهم بعضا فرجعت إلى قول الله عز وجل إن الشيطان لكم عدو", "english": "And it is said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"The scholar who does not act upon his knowledge is like the one who commands the people to perform the prayers seeking the nearness of God while he himself does not seek the nearness of God.\" And it is said: \"The scholar who does not act upon his knowledge is like the one who commands the people to perform the prayers seeking the meeting of God while he himself does not seek the meeting of God.\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 54", "section_title_en": "Section 54", "section_index": 54, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "14": [{"arabic": "وأما الاختلاف فقوله تعالى {قالت الأعراب آمنا قل لم تؤمنوا ولكن قولوا أسلمنا} ومعناه استسلمنا في الظاهر فأراد بالإيمان ههنا التصديق بالقلب فقط", "english": "As for their synonymy, it is in His saying, exalted be He: {فَأَخْرَجْنَا مَن كَانَ فِيهَا مِنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ * فَمَا وَجَدْنَا فِيهَا غَيْرَ بَيْتٍ مِّنَ الْمُسْلِمِينَ} [And We brought out whoever was in it of the believers. But We found not therein other than one house of the Muslims] (Quran 51:35-36) — and by unanimous agreement there was only one house. And He, exalted be He, also said: {يَا قَوْمِ إِن كُنتُمْ آمَنتُم بِاللَّهِ فَعَلَيْهِ تَوَكَّلُوا إِن كُنتُم مُّسْلِمِينَ} [O my people, if you have believed in God, then rely upon Him, if you are Muslims] (Quran 10:84). And the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Islam is built upon five [pillars]\" {بُنِيَ الإِسْلَامُ عَلَى خَمْسٍ}. And the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, was once asked about faith (iman), and he answered with these same five.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 98", "section_title_en": "On Whether Islam and Faith (Iman) Are Identical or Distinct", "section_index": 98, "anchors": "- Root: أمن (amana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes safety, security, and freedom from fear; the isti'man form yields 'belief' as an act of granting credence and trust.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإيمان هو التصديق\" — Faith (iman) is fundamentally assent (tasdiq), a conviction of the heart, not a mere verbal profession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage ties iman to tasdiq (verification/assent); metaphorical extension applies it to outward compliance only in a derived sense.\n- Root: سلم (salima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes soundness, safety, and freedom from defect; the istislam form yields 'submission' as an act of yielding and surrendering.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإسلام عبارة عن التسليم والاستسلام\" — Islam is surrender (taslim) and self-abandonment (istislam) through compliance and obedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage encompasses outward acts of the limbs; metaphorical (Majaz) usage can extend to inward assent, but the primary domain is the external.\n- Root: صدق (sadaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes truthfulness and veracity in speech and report.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول\" — Truth and falsehood originate in speech; tasdiq is the act of affirming a report as true.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is in verbal confirmation; metaphorical extension applies to 'true in resolve' or 'true in attack,' i.e., confirming through action.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the permissible separation between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, relevant here for parsing the conditional and contrastive structures in the theological arguments.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "15": [{"arabic": "فقد قال الله تعالى إنما المؤمنون الذين آمنوا بالله ورسوله ثم لم يرتابوا وجاهدوا بأموالهم وأنفسهم في سبيل الله أولئك هم الصادقون {فيكون}", "english": "And doubt concerning the perfection of faith is rightfully warranted from two angles: first, because hypocrisy (nifāq) removes the perfection of faith, and it is hidden — one cannot ascertain one's own immunity from it. Second, because faith is perfected by acts of obedience, and one does not know whether they exist in their full perfection. As for action, God — exalted is He — has said: {«إِنَّمَا الْمُؤْمِنُونَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بِاللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ ثُمَّ لَمْ يَرْتَابُوا وَجَاهَدُوا بِأَمْوَالِهِمْ وَأَنفُسِهِمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أُولَٰئِكَ هُمُ الصَّادِقُونَ»} \"The believers are only those who believe in God and His Messenger, then do not doubt, and strive with their wealth and their selves in the way of God — those are the truthful ones.\" {فيكون} — and thus it becomes...", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 102", "section_title_en": "On the Meaning of the Salaf's Statement \"I am a Believer, If God Wills\" — Section 102", "section_index": 102, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which is breathed; the innermost reality of a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس is used for the rūḥ (spirit), for the dhāt (essence/self), and for the seat of intention and knowledge. When one says \"he knows in himself (nafsihi) that he is a believer,\" it denotes certitude residing in the very core of one's being, not a corporeal faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage refers to the soul's reflexive self-awareness; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to \"blood\" (dam) or \"the eye\" (ʿayn) of envy. Here, \"in himself\" (fī nafsihi) is literal self-knowledge, not mere outward profession.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Face, countenance; the foremost part; a mode, aspect, or direction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The face is the most noble apparent part of a thing; hence it is used for the \"aspect\" or \"manner\" (wajh) of an affair. \"Four aspects\" (wujūh) means four distinct modes of interpretation or rational grounds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the face of a person or object. Metaphorical: \"the face of the matter\" (wajh al-amr) — its most salient and honorable dimension. Here, the \"aspects\" are hermeneutical angles, not physical parts.\n- Root: ايم (a-y-m)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unmarried state (aym); one who has no spouse. Not applicable here directly, but the root's semantic field of \"being alone/without attachment\" contrasts with īmān's root (a-m-n) — security and trust — which is the operative term in this text.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Not directly relevant; the operative root is أمن (a-m-n), meaning security from fear, and by extension, faith (īmān) as the state of being secure in God's truth.\n- Root: كان (k-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be, to exist; the copula of predication.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kāna denotes existence and becoming; in theological usage, it grounds the distinction between what is (mawjūd) and what is merely asserted. The question \"Are you a believer?\" (a-mu'min anta?) uses kāna implicitly to ask about actual, present state — not future contingency.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: existence. Metaphorical: the \"being-so\" of a predicate. The istithnā' (exception) with \"in shā' Allāh\" suspends the copula's certainty, shifting from assertive existence to contingent hope.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الاستثناء والتعليق بالمشيئة (The Chapter of Exception and Suspension by the Divine Will) — When an exception clause (istithnā') follows a positive assertion, it grammatically suspends the universality or certainty of the predication. In theological discourse, this suspension may be semantic (doubt) or pragmatic (deference, etiquette, or self-deprecation), and Sibawayh's parsing of conditional suspension (taʿlīq) shows that the apodosis need not be negated — only its absolute, unqualified force is tempered.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 40, 2795], [2, 2795, 3917], [3, 3917, 5719], [4, 5719, 6348], [5, 6348, 8057], [6, 8057, 8669], [7, 8669, 9519], [8, 9519, 10199], [9, 10199, 11695], [10, 11695, 13531], [11, 13531, 14211], [12, 14211, 14789], [13, 14789, 15469], [14, 15469, 16447], [15, 16447, 17410]], "49:1": [[1, 40, 2795], [2, 2795, 3917], [3, 3917, 5719], [4, 5719, 6348], [5, 6348, 8057], [6, 8057, 8669], [7, 8669, 9519], [8, 9519, 10199], [9, 10199, 11695], [10, 11695, 13531], [11, 13531, 14211], [12, 14211, 14789], [13, 14789, 15469], [14, 15469, 16447], [15, 16447, 17410]], "2": [[1, 410, 3676], [2, 3676, 5007], [3, 5007, 7144], [4, 7144, 7890], [5, 7890, 10743], [6, 10743, 12423], [7, 12423, 13189], [8, 13189, 13965], [9, 13965, 15447], [10, 15447, 16677], [11, 16677, 18552], [12, 18552, 18935], [13, 18935, 20175], [14, 20175, 20961], [15, 20961, 22070], [16, 22070, 22977], [17, 22977, 23421], [18, 23421, 24298], [19, 24298, 25760], [20, 25760, 27091], [21, 27091, 27837], [22, 27837, 29420]], "49:2": [[1, 410, 3676], [2, 3676, 5007], [3, 5007, 7144], [4, 7144, 7890], [5, 7890, 10743], [6, 10743, 12423], [7, 12423, 13189], [8, 13189, 13965], [9, 13965, 15447], [10, 15447, 16677], [11, 16677, 18552], [12, 18552, 18935], [13, 18935, 20175], [14, 20175, 20961], [15, 20961, 22070], [16, 22070, 22977], [17, 22977, 23421], [18, 23421, 24298], [19, 24298, 25760], [20, 25760, 27091], [21, 27091, 27837], [22, 27837, 29420]], "3": [[1, 1230, 1942], [2, 1942, 3323], [3, 3323, 5235], [4, 5235, 6957], [5, 6957, 7637], [6, 7637, 8788], [7, 8788, 9625], [8, 9625, 11801], [9, 11801, 13182], [10, 13182, 14113], [11, 14113, 14950], [12, 14950, 16520], [13, 16520, 18736], [14, 18736, 19343], [15, 19343, 20630], [16, 20630, 21865], [17, 21865, 23100]], "49:3": [[1, 1230, 1942], [2, 1942, 3323], [3, 3323, 5235], [4, 5235, 6957], [5, 6957, 7637], [6, 7637, 8788], [7, 8788, 9625], [8, 9625, 11801], [9, 11801, 13182], [10, 13182, 14113], [11, 14113, 14950], [12, 14950, 16520], [13, 16520, 18736], [14, 18736, 19343], [15, 19343, 20630], [16, 20630, 21865], [17, 21865, 23100]], "4": [[1, 530, 1138], [2, 1138, 2318], [3, 2318, 4374], [4, 4374, 4732], [5, 4732, 6931], [6, 6931, 8048], [7, 8048, 9156], [8, 9156, 9817], [9, 9817, 